WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
Imagine giving every living creature on Earth a report card

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.560
for its jobs. Oh like a literal grading system.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:13.860
Yeah exactly you grade them on you know doing an earthworm's work to create the food plants need.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.220
Right making the dirt healthy.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:22.440
Exactly and the other job is staying alive on Mars despite the salt and the poison in the Martian dirt.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:29.160
And the wild thing is when you actually look at the scores the absolute best creature managed only about half.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.180
Right just half. So for this deep dive we are looking at the proposed fix for

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.960
this. Which is just a totally wild plan.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:42.380
It really is. We simply borrow every tool the cell is missing from creatures that already have them.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:43.880
Oh like pulling tools from a toolbox?

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:49.820
Yeah exactly and then we pack them all into an ordinary soil bacterium that's already a third of the way there.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.980
Well wait as the friendly skeptic here I have to point out the massive risk with that.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:55.960
Oh definitely.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:02.780
I mean combining all these pieces it's basically like dropping a massive racing engine into a tiny golf cart and just you know.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.060
Expecting it to drive.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:11.400
Exactly you can't just pile on these new tools and expect the cell to survive the strain it would just shake itself apart.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.140
Which brings up the unchecked unknowns.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.940
Right things nobody has ever actually checked.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:21.220
Yeah like for starters does adding life to dead Martian dirt actually create soil at

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.620
all? That is a huge question we just assume it will work.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:28.940
And then does an ability really travel with the gene behind it?

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.040
Right because having the code doesn't guarantee the creature actually knows how to use the tool.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:36.880
But the incredible thing is how simple the tests for this

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.860
are. Oh they are so cheap and easy.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:43.320
Yeah for the dirt you just use cheap cups of Martian dirt with and without worms.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.780
Like comparing batches of a kitchen recipe?

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:51.880
Exactly like that and to test the gene you take a tiny worm bred without that specific gene.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.680
Like pulling a single block from a tower to see if its superpower vanishes?

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.960
Right if your ability disappears you know you found the exact tool you need to

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.960
borrow. But you know we have to deliver the honest verdict here.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:03.920
Yeah we really do.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.600
This simply cannot be built yet.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:06.540
It just can't.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:08.620
The golf cart just can't hold that racing engine.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:16.460
Right which means we have a massive cliffhanger for you. Our next deep dive will cover the exact computer test that broke the design.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.280
It is a wild test to look at.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.920
It really is. Now you can find all the details on the MarsWorm website.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.340
Which you should absolutely check out.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:29.820
For sure. It is a student project by Saanvi for the 3M Young Scientist Challenge 2027.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:31.000
Such a cool project.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:44.740
Yeah and it leaves you with this final thought. If we eventually figure out how to build a microbe to survive the absolute worst conditions on Mars, what could that exact same technology do for the most barren deserts right here on Earth?
